# Probability Arena — CRYPTO-COVERAGE-REPAIR-002 prospective sparse observation.
# Enrols eligible NEW births into the standing rolling cohort and buys exactly
# one 6h and one 24h market/liquidity observation per birth, through the
# DexScreener adapter and nothing else. SolanaTracker is structurally denied by
# a run-scoped provider policy, not merely unused. No cohort arming, no
# discovery scan, no wallets, no keys, no swaps, no signing, no orders, no
# execution of any kind. Measurement only, never advice.
# A complete no-op while ENABLE_CRYPTO_SPARSE_OBSERVATION is false or unset.
# NOT auto-installed. See the .timer file for install commands.
#
# GATE7-SPARSE-UNITS-001 authored these units. NOTHING IS INSTALLED, ENABLED OR
# STARTED BY THAT MILESTONE — these are repo templates and activation is an
# operator action, in the order documented under "Sparse observer timer" in
# docs/EVO_X2_RUNBOOK.md (which leads with the DISABLE procedure, on purpose).

[Unit]
Description=Probability Arena crypto sparse horizon observation (DexScreener only)
# NO After=network-online.target, and for a DIFFERENT reason than the
# reconciler's. The reconciler omits it because it makes zero external calls;
# THIS LANE DOES MAKE THEM, so the honest reason has to be stated rather than
# inherited. `network-online.target` belongs to the SYSTEM manager; a
# `systemd --user` unit ordering against it does not get the dependency it
# looks like it is getting, and writing the line would tell a future reader
# this lane's network readiness is handled when it is not. What actually
# covers a not-yet-up network is the adapter contract plus the band:
# `DexScreenerAdapter._get` never raises on transport failure, `_fetch_phase`
# reads the provider ledger's failure delta and the row is written as
# `request_failed` — which `_is_retryable` re-plans on the NEXT pass while the
# band is still open (SPARSE_MAX_ATTEMPTS = 2). A pass that fires into a dead
# network costs one wasted request per due token and loses nothing.

[Service]
Type=oneshot
WorkingDirectory=%h/projects/probability-arena
EnvironmentFile=%h/projects/probability-arena/.env
# NO `--force` AND NO `--dry-run`. This is not style; both would corrupt the
# record this lane exists to produce:
#   * `--force` sets `gate_bypassed=True` on the per-pass telemetry record.
#     `run_source` is DERIVED from systemd's `INVOCATION_ID` (see
#     `app/telemetry/writer_pass.py`), so every run of this unit files as
#     `run_source="scheduled"`. `scheduled` + `gate_bypassed=True` is defined
#     in that module as a real anomaly — putting `--force` here would file
#     every single timer run as an attended bypass and destroy the one signal
#     that catches a genuine one.
#   * `--dry-run` enrols nothing, fetches nothing and writes nothing, so a
#     timer carrying it would be a schedule that produces no observations
#     while looking green in `systemctl list-timers`.
# The gate is meant to be crossed by the FLAG in .env, never by an argument
# here. See the enable procedure in the runbook.
ExecStart=%h/projects/probability-arena/.venv/bin/python -m app.cli crypto-sparse-observe
NoNewPrivileges=true
# SAFE TO INSTALL DARK, and this is the confirmed behaviour, not an intention.
# With `ENABLE_CRYPTO_SPARSE_OBSERVATION` unset or false and neither bypass
# argument present, `run_scheduled_sparse_observation` returns
# `_base_result(STATUS_DISABLED, ...)` from its FIRST branch: before the
# overlap lock, before the MarketOps health read, before the cohort lookup —
# so no row is read, no row is written, no provider is called and no telemetry
# record is appended (the `disabled` return deliberately does not pass through
# `_finish`, precisely so an hourly dark timer does not file 24 events a day
# describing nothing). The CLI prints
#   status=disabled  external_calls=0  no-op (flag ... is off)
# and returns 0, so the unit goes green. A dark tick costs one Python
# interpreter start and one settings load. That is the entire cost.
#
# Yield CPU and IO. This lane's own write-lock hold is small (25-token batches,
# <= 50 rows per commit, measured max competing-writer stall in the WRITE PHASE
# 45 ms — the same measurement put the PRELUDE's max at 2,023 ms), but it
# shares the host with a continuously-writing watcher, a 5-minute MarketOps
# timer and a documented lock-contention history, and the production database
# runs `journal_mode=delete` — where a reader and a writer exclude each other,
# so even this lane's prelude SELECTs are contention, not free. The live lanes
# must win every scheduling contest against a measurement lane.
Nice=10
CPUWeight=20
IOWeight=20
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# TimeoutStartSec — DERIVED HERE, NOT COPIED FROM THE RECONCILER
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The reconciler's 7min is its own number from its own arithmetic and means
# nothing here: different phases, different bounds, and this lane has no
# lock-wait budget, so its per-lock-acquisition figure is the full inherited
# `sqlite_busy_timeout_ms` (30 s) rather than a derived 5 s share.
#
# The pass decomposes into a LOAD-INDEPENDENT part N and a LOCK-DEPENDENT part.
# Write the per-lock-acquisition wait as w and the host's SQLite busy-timeout
# overshoot factor as L (measured in this repo: 1.01x on an idle EVO, 5.80x on
# a dev Mac at load average 5-6; no constant bounds it).
#
#   total(w, L) = N + B * w * L
#
# N, the terms that involve no lock at all:
#
#   interpreter start + import — `TimeoutStartSec` on a
#     `Type=oneshot` bounds the WHOLE process, not just the pass,
#     and `python -m app.cli` imports SQLAlchemy, pydantic and
#     httpx before `run_scheduled_sparse_observation` exists.
#     Measured on the dev Mac at load average 3-4: 0.41 / 0.44 /
#     0.67 s wall for a bare `import app.cli` (of which
#     `-X importtime` attributes 1.39 s cumulative to warm
#     imports). Allowance ~4.5x the worst measurement:                    3 s
#   prelude compute — measured 110 ms of a 1,569 ms pass at
#     193k members, cold cache (warm `_enrolment_candidates`
#     1.34 ms, `_sparse_plan` 18.26 ms). Allowance 9x the
#     measurement:                                                       1 s
#   fetch phase — `crypto_sparse_observation_max_duration_seconds`
#     default 90 s, ANCHORED AT FETCH START (`_fetch_phase`'s
#     docstring), so the prelude is not inside it; plus one
#     in-flight request, because the deadline is checked only
#     BETWEEN tokens and the loop can begin a fetch at
#     deadline-epsilon. One `DexScreenerAdapter` request carries
#     a 15 s httpx timeout, which is PER OPERATION, not per
#     request: `Timeout(15.0)` sets connect, write, read AND pool
#     to 15 s each, and a request that spends 14.9 s in each
#     phase it actually performs trips none of them. The
#     in-flight fetch is therefore charged at 3 x 15 s — connect,
#     write, read. POOL IS NOT CHARGED, and that is the one
#     judgement in this line: the adapter issues its requests
#     sequentially against a client whose pool is never
#     contended, so acquisition does not queue. A 4th 15 s would
#     make the figure 180 s and would still not move the verdict.
#                                                    90 + 45  =         135 s
#   write-phase retry sleeps — 4 batches (observe_limit 100 /
#     write_batch_size 25) x 2 sleeps x DB_LOCKED_RETRY_SECONDS
#     3.0 s. These are `time.sleep`, so no L applies:                   24 s
#   teardown + the single JSONL telemetry append (non-SQLite,
#     no lock, one `os.write`):                                          1 s
#   ------------------------------------------------------------------------
#   N                                                                  164 s
#
# B, the number of points at which this pass can wait on the SQLite lock. All
# three counts are STRUCTURALLY bounded by shipped constants, not by hope:
#
#   prelude SELECTs (MarketOps health, cohort lookup, migration-0029 index
#     probe, `_enrolment_candidates`, `_sparse_plan`) — under
#     `journal_mode=delete` a read waits on a writer's exclusive lock, so
#     these are blocking points. Generous count:                          8
#   enrolment commits — ceil(enrol_limit 200 / write_batch_size 25).
#     `_enrol` deliberately has NO retry ladder (a lock refuses the pass and
#     the next pass re-selects; enrolment is idempotent by unique index), so
#     each commit is exactly one acquisition:                             8
#   write-phase preloads (`members`, `retry_rows`):                       2
#   write-phase ladder — 4 batches x DB_LOCKED_MAX_ATTEMPTS 3 x 2
#     acquisitions per attempt (`prepare()` may emit SQL inside the try,
#     then `session.commit()`):                                          24
#   ------------------------------------------------------------------------
#   B                                                                    42
#
# Evaluated:
#
#   w = 0.045 s (this lane's MEASURED max competing-writer stall in its WRITE
#                PHASE — see the citation note below, it is NOT the pass max)
#     L = 1.01x  (EVO, idle, measured)                              ~165.9 s
#     L = 5.80x  (dev Mac at load average 5-6, measured)            ~175.0 s
#
#   w = 30 s (the full inherited busy timeout — the ARITHMETIC CEILING)
#     L = 1.01x                                                    ~1,437 s
#     L = 5.80x                                                    ~7,472 s
#
# NO VALUE OF TimeoutStartSec CAN COVER THE ARITHMETIC CEILING, and this unit
# does not pretend otherwise. At the worst L this repo has measured the ceiling
# is ~124 minutes — longer than the lane's own 60-minute cadence — so a timeout
# that covered it would be a timeout that never fires. The value below is
# therefore chosen against the MEASURED regime, with the exceedance conditions
# written down so the next reader does not have to re-derive them.
#
# 5min (300 s) is 1.81x the measured-regime figure at L = 1.01 and 1.71x at the
# worst measured L = 5.80. That margin is spent entirely on w, the one input the
# measurement does not bound. THE CONDITIONS AT WHICH 300 s IS EXCEEDED:
#
#   holding L = 1.01, at w = 3.21 s   (71.3x the measured 45 ms write-phase stall)
#   holding L = 5.80, at w = 0.56 s   (12.4x the measured 45 ms write-phase stall)
#   holding w = 45 ms, at L = 72.0    (12.4x the worst L ever measured here)
#
# WHERE THE 45 ms COMES FROM, AND WHAT IT IS NOT. Its source is
# `tests/test_crypto_coverage_repair_002.py` (B7, the query-plan test): phase-
# attributed against a real competing writer at 193k members, "the prelude was
# 11.8s of a 14.3s pass with a 2,023ms competing-writer max and 2 hard lock
# failures, while the fetch phase's max was 28ms and the WRITE phase's 45ms."
# 45 ms is therefore the SMALLEST of three phase figures, and the pass-level max
# in that same measurement was 2,023 ms in the PRELUDE — which is consistent
# with the [Service] note above that this lane's prelude SELECTs are contention
# under `journal_mode=delete`, not free. It is quoted here because it is the
# phase w is meant to describe in the B term's largest contributor (the 24
# write-phase acquisitions), and because the prelude's own 8 acquisitions are
# carried at the same w only as an admitted simplification. Anyone re-deriving
# this number should re-derive w per phase rather than inherit one figure.
#
# None of that is a guarantee and must not be quoted as one. But w and the
# in-flight fetch are unbounded in DIFFERENT ways, and the difference is the
# point of the paragraph below:
#
#   * THE LOCK HAS A NOMINAL CEILING. `sqlite_busy_timeout_ms` (30 s) is a
#     contract SQLite makes about ONE acquisition, so B * 30 s is a real — if
#     operationally useless — arithmetic ceiling, tabulated above. What has no
#     ceiling is L, the factor by which a loaded host OVERSHOOTS that contract:
#     1.01x idle, 5.80x at load average 5-6, and nothing bounds it above. The
#     lock term is a bounded quantity INFLATED by an unbounded one.
#   * THE IN-FLIGHT FETCH HAS NO NOMINAL CEILING AT ALL. httpx's 15 s timeout is
#     per socket OPERATION, so a provider that trickles bytes below the read
#     timeout never trips it, and `_fetch_phase` checks its deadline only
#     BETWEEN tokens — nothing in this lane interrupts a request that has
#     already started. There is no contract here to overshoot; there is no
#     contract. For that failure mode TimeoutStartSec IS the only bound, which
#     is a second, independent reason not to set it generously.
#
# THIS VALUE IS DERIVED FROM FOUR SHIPPED CONSTANTS and is invalid without them:
# `DEFAULT_MAX_DURATION_SECONDS` 90.0, `DEFAULT_ENROL_LIMIT` 200,
# `DEFAULT_OBSERVE_LIMIT` 100, `DEFAULT_WRITE_BATCH_SIZE` 25 (all in
# app/services/crypto_sparse_observation.py), plus `DB_LOCKED_MAX_ATTEMPTS` 3 /
# `DB_LOCKED_RETRY_SECONDS` 3.0 (crypto_tape.py) and the adapter's 15 s timeout.
# The deployment plan's step 3 explicitly tells the operator to RE-SET
# `CRYPTO_SPARSE_OBSERVATION_MAX_DURATION_SECONDS` from a measured attended
# pass; doing that invalidates this number. `tests/test_gate7_sparse_units_001.py`
# recomputes the model from those constants and fails if this value stops
# covering it — it is a pin, not a comment.
#
# WHAT HAPPENS WHEN IT IS HIT (worked out for THIS lane, and it is not the
# reconciler's answer). systemd sends SIGTERM and then SIGKILL; Python's default
# SIGTERM disposition terminates without running `finally` blocks.
#   * DURABLE: every enrolment batch whose commit returned, and every write
#     batch whose `_commit_with_retry` returned. Counters advance only after a
#     commit RETURNS, so nothing was ever over-claimed.
#   * LOST: the in-flight batch (staged rows are rolled back when the OS closes
#     the connection), the printed receipt, and — the one that matters — THE
#     TELEMETRY RECORD. `_emit_pass_telemetry` runs inside `_finish`, at the end
#     of the pass, so a killed pass appends NOTHING to the 001A JSONL sink.
#   * THEREFORE THIS LANE HAS NO ORPHAN SIGNATURE. The reconciler's overrun is
#     self-reporting: it leaves a `status='running'` row that
#     `lock_wait_run_row_orphaned()` names. This lane has no run table (the
#     GATE7-SPARSE-TELEMETRY-001 gate lifted only the JSONL half), so an overrun
#     is INVISIBLE IN ITS OWN TELEMETRY and is visible only in
#     `journalctl` (`Failed with result 'timeout'`) and, one band later, as a
#     rising `scheduling_miss_rate` in `crypto-observation-coverage-report`.
#     An operator who suspects an overrun must read the journal; the corpus will
#     not tell them.
#   * NOTHING IS LEFT INCONSISTENT. The overlap lock is a kernel-held `flock`,
#     released on process death, so no stale lock survives. Double-enrolment is
#     impossible (`ix_horizon_member_cohort_token`) and double-observation is
#     impossible (`ix_horizon_obs_cohort_token_horizon`), so the next pass
#     re-plans from persisted state and picks up exactly what has no row yet.
#     There is nothing to repair by hand.
#   * WHAT IS ACTUALLY PAID: the provider requests already spent on the
#     in-flight batch's tokens (at most 25 tokens, and up to the full
#     observe_limit of 100 if the kill lands before the first commit), plus any
#     member-horizon whose band closes before the next pass — which becomes a
#     permanent, never-backfilled `scheduling_miss`.
TimeoutStartSec=5min

# NO [Install] SECTION, AND THIS DEVIATES FROM THE RECONCILER'S SERVICE UNIT ON
# PURPOSE. That unit carries `[Install] WantedBy=default.target`; a timer-driven
# `Type=oneshot` has no use for one, because the TIMER is the thing an operator
# enables and the timer's own `Unit=` is what pulls this service in. Carrying an
# `[Install]` here buys nothing and creates a latent hazard: a stray
# `systemctl --user enable probability-arena-crypto-sparse-observe.service`
# (easy to type when you meant `.timer`, and `systemctl --user list-unit-files`
# will not distinguish the intent) installs a `default.target` want, and this
# lane then runs a full PROVIDER-SPENDING pass at every login, off the schedule,
# with no `OnCalendar` governing it and invariant (2)'s lattice argument silent
# about the extra points. Precedent is not a justification for propagating an
# unsafe default into a lane the precedent did not consider — the reconciler is
# provider-free and this lane is not. `tests/test_gate7_sparse_units_001.py`
# asserts this section's ABSENCE, so restoring it fails the suite.
